      {
        "id": "p55-q24-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Examine the behaviour of $y$ and $y'$ as $x\\to0^+$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y\\to0^-$ and $y'\\to0^-$. The graph approaches the excluded origin from below and becomes horizontal.",
          "worked": "Put $t=-\\ln x$, so $x=e^{-t}$ and $t\\to\\infty$ as $x\\to0^+$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}y&=-te^{-2t}\\\\y'&=(1-2t)e^{-t}\\end{aligned}$$\nExponential decay dominates the linear factors, so both expressions tend to zero. Both are negative for sufficiently large $t$. Thus $y\\to0^-$ and $y'\\to0^-$: the curve approaches the origin from below with gradient tending to zero. The origin is excluded from the domain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 21
      },
      {
        "id": "p55-q24-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Hence sketch the graph of this function, then write down its range.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "See graph. Range: $y\\ge-\\frac1{2e}$.",
          "worked": "The domain is $x>0$, with an open endpoint at the origin. There is no $y$-intercept.\n$$\\begin{aligned}x^2\\ln x&=0\\\\\\ln x&=0\\\\x&=1\\end{aligned}$$\nThe curve decreases to the minimum $\\left(e^{-1/2},-\\frac1{2e}\\right)$, then increases without bound. It is concave down for $0<x<e^{-3/2}$ and concave up for $x>e^{-3/2}$. As $x\\to0^+$, it approaches the origin horizontally from below. Hence the range is $\\left[-\\frac1{2e},\\infty\\right)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
            {
              "id": "p55-q24-graph",
